\paragraph{Adaptive QR unlocked by high-bandwidth memory.}
A qualitatively different result appears in the Adaptive mode comparison.
On GPU-B, Adaptive and Householder run at essentially the same speed (within 5\%,
both bandwidth-saturated); on GPU-C, Adaptive is \textbf{22--35\% faster} than
Householder on the same chip, and \textbf{36--41\% faster} than GPU-B Adaptive.
The mechanism: Cholesky-QR (Adaptive's primary branch) is an $O(\chi^3)$
triangular solve versus Householder's $O(\chi^3)$ full QR, but Cholesky has a
smaller constant and lower memory traffic per contraction.
On GPU-B, where HBM keeps both modes bandwidth-saturated, this constant factor
is invisible.
On GPU-C, high-bandwidth memory delivers data fast enough that the compute bottleneck is exposed;
Cholesky's lower constant then translates directly into wall-clock savings.
This is a hardware--software co-design effect: the same algorithm choice that
appeared neutral on GPU-B yields a $\mathbf{1.4\times}$ improvement on GPU-C.

The $\sim 1.6\times$ total Adaptive speedup (GPU-C vs.\ GPU-B) decomposes into
${\sim}1.2\times$ from architecture alone (high-bandwidth memory bandwidth, visible in
Householder-vs-Householder) and ${\sim}1.3\times$ from the interaction of
algorithm choice and memory subsystem (Cholesky FLOPS unlocked when bandwidth
pressure is relieved).

