\subsection*{Spatial operator spreading at $10\times10$}

At large grid sizes the autocorrelator $C(t)$ decays to zero by quantum scrambling
--- the initial operator $Z_c$ disperses its weight across the full lattice, so the
overlap with the identity at the center site vanishes.
This is \emph{correct physics}, not a numerical failure: the light cone has
saturated and the operator has fully spread.
In this regime, $C(t)$ alone is uninformative about the dynamics; the physically
interesting quantity is \emph{how} the operator weight dispersed.

We characterize spreading with three metrics extracted from the per-site Pauli
weight map $n(\mathbf{r}, t)$ (recorded by the \texttt{--measure=all} flag):
\begin{align}
 R_{L1}(t) &= \frac{\sum_{\mathbf{r}} n(\mathbf{r},t)\,
 \|\mathbf{r}-\mathbf{r}_c\|_1}{\sum_{\mathbf{r}} n(\mathbf{r},t)},
 \label{eq:rl1} \\
 R_{L2}(t) &= \sqrt{\frac{\sum_{\mathbf{r}} n(\mathbf{r},t)\,
 \|\mathbf{r}-\mathbf{r}_c\|_2^2}{\sum_{\mathbf{r}} n(\mathbf{r},t)}},
 \label{eq:rl2} \\
 S_{\rm frac}(t) &= \frac{-\sum_{\mathbf{r}} p(\mathbf{r},t)\ln p(\mathbf{r},t)}
 {\ln N^2},
 \quad p(\mathbf{r},t) = \frac{n(\mathbf{r},t)}{\sum n},
 \label{eq:sfrac}
\end{align}
where $\mathbf{r}_c$ is the center site and $N^2$ is the total number of sites.
$S_{\rm frac}$ is the Shannon entropy of the weight distribution normalized to
$[0,1]$: $S_{\rm frac}=0$ corresponds to a perfectly localized operator;
$S_{\rm frac}=1$ corresponds to uniform weight on all sites (fully scrambled).

